# {{.Name}} ({{.Type}})

> **Deployment recommendation:** Use Terraform for databases, service accounts,
> access tokens, Duckling configuration, roles/grants, and shares. Keep Dives,
> Flight Python, and Guide Markdown in version control and deploy them through
> the MotherDuck CLI or a code deployment pipeline. Terraform apply does not
> compile application code, validate every data dependency, or run a data load.

See [resource scope and ownership migration](../guides/resource-scope.md).

Manage a Flight's Python definition and optional schedule when Terraform is its
sole deployment owner. For application packages with previews and promotion,
use [Blueprints](../guides/blueprints-deployment.md).

## Definition and execution

`source_code` and `requirements_txt` can read version-controlled files with
`file(...)`. `config` supplies string runtime configuration. Keep credentials
out of those values and use the supported Flight secret mechanisms.

Creating or updating the definition does not mean that a data load has run or
succeeded. Configure `schedule_cron` for recurring execution, or trigger a run
through the application deployment workflow. The Flight-run Terraform resource
is deprecated.

## Plan limits

MotherDuck applies Flight limits by plan and rejects a create or update that
exceeds them:

| Plan | Default `max_runtime_sec` | Maximum `max_runtime_sec` | Schedules |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Business | 28,800 (8 hours) | No cap. `0` removes the limit. | Yes |
| Free trial | 28,800 (8 hours) | 28,800 | Yes |
| Lite | 3,600 (1 hour) | 3,600 | Yes |
| Free | 3,600 (1 hour) | 3,600 | No |

On a plan with a cap, `max_runtime_sec = 0` is rejected. The provider cannot
see the organization's plan, so these limits fail at apply time, not at plan
time.

MotherDuck can disable a schedule on its own, for example after the
organization moves to a plan without scheduled runs. `schedule_status` then
reads `DISABLED` while `schedule_cron` keeps its value. Terraform reports the
status but does not manage it.

The provider validates at plan time that `source_code` is 1 to 204,800 bytes,
that `requirements_txt` is at most 20,480 bytes, and that `config` does not use
a reserved name: `MOTHERDUCK_TOKEN`, `MOTHERDUCK_FLIGHTS_RUN`,
`MOTHERDUCK_FLIGHT_ID`, or `MOTHERDUCK_FLIGHT_RUN_ID`.

## Lifecycle and import

Import by Flight UUID to preserve identity. Terraform reads the current
definition and then reconciles configured code and settings. Do not edit the
same Flight through both Terraform and another deployment system.

Destroy deletes the Flight definition. Verify its schedules, dependencies, and
data-retention requirements before removing it. Function availability depends
on the MotherDuck account and client.

See [resource scope](../guides/resource-scope.md).

